= Solution
{parent=2}

Nonrelativistic acceleration from rest gives $p=\sqrt{2m_eeV}$ and $\lambda=h/p$. The observed deflection is the angle between incident and diffracted beams, **$2\theta=22^\circ$**, so <Bragg's law> at first order gives
$$
\boxed{d=\frac{h}{2\sin11^\circ\sqrt{2m_ee(150\,\mathrm V)}}\simeq2.62\times10^{-10}\,\mathrm m=0.262\,\mathrm{nm}.}
$$
Here $\lambda\simeq0.100\,\mathrm{nm}$. The numerical evaluation uses the externally documented CODATA 2014 values $h=6.626070040\times10^{-34}\,\mathrm{J\,s}$, $m_e=9.10938356\times10^{-31}\,\mathrm{kg}$ and $e=1.6021766208\times10^{-19}\,\mathrm C$; it does not assert which rounded constants were in the exam's handbook. Relativistic corrections at this voltage are negligible at the precision of the angle.<CODATA recommended values of the fundamental physical constants 2014 by National Institute of Standards and Technology>[Planck constant, elementary charge and electron mass in the historical reference table]{ref}

= 3
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

If $\hat A|a\rangle=\lambda|a\rangle$, the <Hermitian operator> property makes $\lambda$ real. Applying the operator identity to this <eigenvector> gives $\lambda^3=1$. Its only real root is **$\lambda=1$**; the other cube roots of unity are nonreal. In an orthonormal <eigenbasis>, $\hat A=\sum_a|a\rangle\langle a|=\boxed{\hat I}$. Equivalently, the <spectral theorem> places the entire spectrum of the self-adjoint observable at one, giving the same conclusion. The quantum-mechanical setting assumes the usual well-defined observable and operator domains.

= 4
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

At the central values, $z=\sqrt{\tan45^\circ}+0.10^2=1.010$. For independent input standard errors, linear propagation gives
$$
\sigma_z^2=\left(\frac{\sec^2x}{2\sqrt{\tan x}}\sigma_x\right)^2+(2y\sigma_y)^2.
$$
The angular derivative here is with respect to radians, so $\sigma_x=2\pi/180=0.03491$. At $x=\pi/4$, the derivative is one, and
$$
\sigma_z=\sqrt{(0.03491)^2+(0.2\times0.02)^2}=0.0351.
$$
Thus **$\boxed{z=1.010\pm0.035}$**, with the stated <measurement uncertainty> interpreted as one <standard error>. Correlated inputs would require a <covariance> term; their correlation is not supplied.

= 5
{parent=Section A}
